## What do you think separates a Senior Software Engineer role from a Software Engineer?

Scope of ownership and judgment, not years served or syntax known. A software engineer is given a problem and solves it well. A senior engineer steps back first. Is this the right problem to solve, and which parts are better left alone? What does the work affect downstream, and who feels it six months from now? The seniority is in the altitude you can operate at, not the lines you write.

That wider view shows up in concrete ways. First, judgment under ambiguity, knowing when to ship lean and carry a little debt on purpose, and when that debt will quietly hurt you later. Second, ownership past the merge, the tests, the docs, the on-call, the production reality, not just the pull request. Third, multiplying the people around you through review, design input, and leaving the next engineer's job easier rather than only finishing your own.

The common thread is accountability for outcomes, not just output. A senior engineer keeps one eye on the immediate task and one on the broader system, the business, and the people the work actually affects, and is trusted to make that call without being told.
